<BaseLayout title="Intro to Shading — Supply List">
  <div class="home">
    <h3 class="page-category">Online workshop</h3>
    <h1>Intro to Shading</h1>
    <h2>Supply list</h2>

    <h4>Paper</h4>
    <p>Any good quality drawing paper will work. You want some texture, but not too much. For this workshop, a 9×12 pad will be good, though you could go a little bigger or smaller.</p>
    <p>Recommendation: <a href="https://www.dickblick.com/items/strathmore-400-series-drawing-paper-pad-9-x-12-24-sheets/">Strathmore 400, 80lb, medium texture</a></p>

    <h4>Pencils</h4>
    <p>You want a range of pencils from soft to hard. The brand isn't super important, as long as they're decent quality artist pencils.</p>
    <p>Recommendation: <a href="https://www.dickblick.com/products/staedtler-lumograph-drawing-sketching-pencils/">Staedtler Lumograph HB, H, 2H, 4H</a></p>

    <h4>Eraser</h4>
    <p>We just want a regular kneaded eraser that's fairly firm. Unfortunately the quality of a lot of brands of kneaded erasers has really dropped lately and they're super squishy or sticky.</p>
    <p>Recommendation: <a href="https://www.dickblick.com/products/blick-kneaded-erasers/">Blick kneaded eraser</a></p>
    <p>Optional: A pen-style eraser like the <a href="https://www.dickblick.com/items/tombow-mono-zero-refillable-eraser-round-single/">Tombow Mono Zero Round</a></p>

    <h4>Sharpener</h4>
    <p>We'll be getting our pencils super sharp! Most manual and electric sharpeners don't get quite sharp enough. Check out the videos on <a href="https://timdose.notion.site/Shading-workshop-851484873f924f9982b045073c83f621">this page</a> for a few different methods.</p>

    <h4>Board</h4>
    <p>Any drawing board will do!</p>

    <h4>Easel</h4>
    <p>I <strong>highly recommend</strong> working upright. It makes it much easier to use lighter pressure to make lighter values.</p>
    <p>Any easel will do. If you don't have an easel, see if you can find a wall or something else upright to work on.</p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
